Verbolia's reverse proxy solution allows eCommerce sites to create new pages by routing traffic through a proxy server. This can be useful for implementing features such as A/B testing, caching, and load balancing.

To integrate with Verbolia's reverse proxy solution, you will first need to set up a proxy server. This can be done using a variety of technologies, such as NGINX or Apache. Once your proxy server is set up, you can configure it to route traffic to the appropriate backend server for each new page that you want to create.

Here are a few examples of how you might set up your proxy server to integrate with Verbolia's reverse proxy solution:

**NGINX example:**
```
upstream backend {
  server backend1.example.com;
  server backend2.example.com;
  server backend3.example.com;
}

server {
  listen 80;

  location /newpage1 {
    proxy_pass http://backend;
  }

  location /newpage2 {
    proxy_pass http://backend;
  }
}
```
In this example, we set up an `upstream` block to define the backend servers that will handle traffic for our new pages. We then use the `proxy_pass` directive in the `location` block for each new page to route traffic to the appropriate backend server.

**Apache example:**
```
<Proxy balancer://backend>
  BalancerMember http://backend1.example.com
  BalancerMember http://backend2.example.com
  BalancerMember http://backend3.example.com
</Proxy>

<Location /newpage1>
  ProxyPass balancer://backend
</Location>

<Location /newpage2>
  ProxyPass balancer://backend
</Location>
```
In this example, we use the `Proxy` and `Location` directives to define the backend servers and route traffic to them for each new page. We use the `balancer://` scheme to enable load balancing, which allows the proxy server to distribute traffic evenly across the backend servers.

By using Verbolia's reverse proxy solution and configuring your proxy server appropriately, you can easily create new pages for your eCommerce site and take advantage of advanced features such as caching and load balancing.

